    skos:editorialNote "Encyclopaedia Judaica (2007), 'Midrash Tehillim', on Jewish Virtual Library: Zunz: edited in Italy in the last centuries of the geonic period; Buber: ancient and Palestinian; Albeck: groups of midrashim composed over 'some centuries'; the concluding section dates to the 13th century.",
        "Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Midrash' (M. D. Herr), on Jewish Virtual Library: Its table puts Midrash Tehillim I in the middle period (640–1000; the Encyclopedia.com rendering shows the 900–1000 block) and Midrash Tehillim II in the late period (11th–12th centuries).",
        "Wikipedia (English), 'Midrash Tehillim' (cites Friedeman & Cuénod 2021): Psalms 1–118 reached final form between the 7th and 9th centuries; Psalms 119–150 were compiled in the 13th century; edited in Palestine, and Zunz's Italy thesis is rejected." ;
    skos:note "It is a layered work: Buber argued for an early Palestinian origin, Zunz for redaction in Italy late in the geonic period, and Albeck for a gradual growth over several centuries; the supplement on Psalms 119–150 is a 13th-century addition."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "החלק העיקרי ארץ-ישראלי (לשון, אמוראים); צונץ הציע איטליה; ההשלמה לפרקים קיט–קנ מאוחרת (אולי ר' מתתיה היצהרי מסרגוסה)" ;
