opp:dating_Tanna_Debei_Eliyahu_Rabbah a opp:DatingAssertion,
        time:ProperInterval ;
    rdfs:label "המאה ה-8–ה-10 (לפי רוב הדעות; הוצעו תאריכים מהמאה ה-3 עד ה-10)"@he ;
    opp:confidence opp:conf_low ;
    dcterms:date "0700/1000" ;
    dcterms:source opp:source_792a7cc5de,
        opp:source_d4a1b73550,
        opp:source_fa7d0ccacb ;
    dcterms:spatial opp:region_08 ;
    skos:editorialNote "Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Midrash' (M. D. Herr), on Jewish Virtual Library: Its table puts Tanna de-Vei Eliyahu in the 640–900 block of the middle period.",
        "Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Tanna de-Vei Eliyahu' (Jacob Elbaum), on Encyclopedia.com: The date and place are unresolved, with proposals 'almost anywhere between the third and tenth centuries' (Rapoport and Zunz: 10th; Friedmann and Margalioth: 3rd; Mann and Epstein: end of the amoraic era; Aptowitzer: 9th); Albeck: compiled before the 9th century.",
        "Wikipedia (English), 'Tanna Devei Eliyahu' (Jewish Encyclopedia-based): Composed around the end of the 10th century (internal chronology points to 968–984); place debated: Babylonia (Friedmann), Palestine, or Italy/Rome (Güdemann, Derenbourg, Grätz)." ;
    skos:note "It has been dated 'almost anywhere between the third and tenth centuries' (Elbaum, EJ): Friedmann and Margalioth date the core to the 3rd century; Mann and Epstein to the end of the amoraic era; Aptowitzer to the 9th century; Rapoport and Zunz to the 10th (internal reckoning points to 968–984). Albeck holds only that it is certainly before the 9th century, and its place (Babylonia, Palestine or Italy) is disputed."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "הוצעו בבל (פרידמן), ארץ ישראל, איטליה/רומי (גידמן, דרנבורג, גרץ); לא הוכרע" ;
    time:hasBeginning opp:dating_Tanna_Debei_Eliyahu_Rabbah_begin ;
    time:hasEnd opp:dating_Tanna_Debei_Eliyahu_Rabbah_end .

