    skos:editorialNote "M. van Loopik, The Ways of the Sages and the Way of the World (1991), preface/introduction (Mohr Siebeck PDF): DEZ I–III(IV) roughly dates to Tannaitic times, but the final editing presumably took place in Gaonitic times.",
        "Sefaria, 'Tractate Derekh Eretz Zuta' (About this text): Composed c. 100 BCE – c. 900 CE.",
        "Wikipedia (English), 'Derekh Eretz Zutta' (based on the Jewish Encyclopedia): Sections 5–8 are by one editor after the completion of the Babylonian Talmud; 'one would not be far wrong in setting the 9th century as the date of composition'; sections 1–4 much earlier; Perek ha-Shalom very late." ;
    skos:note "Chapters 1–4 may be a tannaitic collection while chapters 5–8 were edited after the Bavli; the 9th-century dating is inferred from Halakhot Gedolot, and van Loopik puts the final editing in the Geonic period."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "פרקים א-ד אולי תנאיים; פרקים ה-ח מעורך אחד שהכיר את הבבלי; 'פרק השלום' מאוחר מאוד" ;
